\section{The seed covariance}{


SIR starts from a proposal built from a \emph{seed} covariance. By default
(\code{seedCov = NULL}) this is the covariance installed on the fit, such as the
\code{"r,s"} or \code{"analytic"} covariance the estimation computed. When \code{"sir"} is
installed itself, the seed that SIR covariance was computed from is used
again, so SIR is never seeded from its own result.

If the fit has no covariance at all, the seed is a diagonal proposal built
from relative standard errors (\code{rseTheta}, by default 30\%), as in
\code{runSIRControl(rseTheta = 30)}. This is an assumed uncertainty, not an
estimated one, and \code{setCov()} says so when it uses it.

A SIR covariance is cached on the fit like any other \code{setCov()}
covariance. It is reused only when both these options and the seed
covariance are the same as when it was computed. Changing the installed
covariance (for example with \code{setCov(fit, "analytic")}) changes the default
seed, so a later \code{setCov(fit, "sir")} recomputes.
}

